# THE INSTANCE, AND ITS MODE #

# The instance is named in the program text rather than derived from the #
# expression, and that is the whole point: the adapton store it holds #
# survives every edit that leaves the name alone. #

# WHAT THE NAME AFTER `as` IS #

# It reads like a variable and it is not one.  Nothing in the Hazel #
# program around it binds `store`, and nothing in the Fumola program #
# inside it binds `store` either -- put the cursor on it and Hazel says #
# `Instance Name`, not `Variable Reference`. #

# It is a key written into the program text.  The runtime keeps a table of #
# VM instances; the name is looked up in that table, and an instance is #
# created the first time a name is seen and found by that name ever after. #
# So the same name in two programs means one instance and one store, a #
# different name means a different store, and nothing an edit does to the #
# program around it can change which store the program is talking to. #

# That is the difference from a variable worth holding onto: a variable #
# denotes whatever its binder currently says, so editing the binder #
# changes it.  This name denotes the same instance until you retype the #
# name itself. #

# `:=` puts a value at a name and hands back a POINTER to it.  `@` reads a #
# pointer.  So a put and a read are two steps, and the pointer is what #
# joins them: #

let a = fumola $graphical as store in
  { let p = 0 := 41; @ p }
end in

# A second program naming `store` runs against the same instance, so this #
# put lands on the cell the first one made rather than on a fresh cell. #
# The Fumola panel shows that as a realignment, not a new node: #

let b = fumola $graphical as store in
  { let q = 0 := 42; @ q }
end in

# A different name is a different instance, with nothing in it: #

let elsewhere = fumola $graphical as other in
  { let r = 0 := 1; @ r }
end in

# The mode is $graphical or $simple.  Only a $graphical instance records the #
# events the panel shows; a $simple one keeps no graph. #

# Changing a mode RESETS the instance, discarding the store.  That is why #
# the mode is written beside the name, and why leaving the slot as a hole #
# means "leave this instance alone" rather than "use the default". #

(a, b, elsewhere)
